From 9b1a4be53f5b5455c52dfa14925cb8dc76deb8da Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Adhemerval Zanella <adhemerval.zanella@linaro.org>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 15:19:08 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 064/116] UPSTREAM: io: Fix fts_set FTS_FOLLOW in a physical
 walk (BZ 34775)

The fts implementation imported from gnulib in glibc 2.44 opens
directories with O_NOFOLLOW in an FTS_PHYSICAL walk, except for command
line arguments with FTS_COMFOLLOW.  It does not take into account
entries that the application asked to follow with fts_set (FTS_FOLLOW),
fts_read stats the target of the symbolic link and returns it as FTS_D,
but opening it then fails with ENOTDIR, so it is returned again as
FTS_DNR and its contents are never visited.  Without FTS_NOCHDIR, the
descriptor that fts keeps to return from the followed directory is also
leaked.

Mark the entries followed with FTS_FOLLOW with a new private fts_flags
bit (FTS_FOLLOWED).  FTS_SYMFOLLOW can not be used because it is only set
when fts changes the working directory, and it implies a valid
fts_symfd.  The directory of such an entry is opened without O_NOFOLLOW,
both in fts_build and in fts_safe_changedir.  Since O_NOFOLLOW no longer
protects against the directory being replaced between the stat and the
open, fts_safe_changedir checks its device and inode as it already does
for FTS_LOGICAL.  The fts implementation in glibc 2.43 always did this
check.

The test covers the default mode, FTS_NOCHDIR (as used by OpenSCAP,
where the issue was found), and FTS_CWDFD, following the link either
from the FTS_SL entry or from the fts_children list before fts_read
reaches it.

gnulib has the same issue since 2010.

Checked on x86_64-linux-gnu and i686-linux-gnu.

Reported-by: Matthew Burket <mburket@redhat.com>
Reviewed-by: Collin Funk <collin.funk1@gmail.com>

(cherry picked from commit eb897a3dd582e2a6d092e2efa617bb05d0b6e6f9)
